.page timezone 5 the time zone

# DESCRIPTION
The system clock counts seconds since 1970 in UTC; what a program shows is that time moved into a **time zone**. The zone is taken from the environment variable `TZ`, else the first line of `/Hakurei/etc/timezone`, else it is UTC. The kernel command line word `tz=ZONE` makes `init` set `TZ` for every session.

The syntax is the POSIX one:

    NAME OFFSET [DSTNAME [OFFSET] [,START[/TIME],END[/TIME]]]

NAME is three or more letters (or anything in angle brackets); OFFSET is hours, with optional `:minutes`, **west** of Greenwich (so Japan is `-9`). Rules look like `Mm.w.d`: month m, week w (5 means the last), weekday d (0 is Sunday); the time defaults to 02:00 local. With a DST name and no rules the United States ones are used.

    UTC
    JST-9
    EST5EDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0
    CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3
    AEST-10AEDT,M10.1.0,M4.1.0/3

A zone that cannot be understood means UTC. `localtime`, `mktime`, `strftime` (`%Z`, `%z`), date(1), `ls -l` and the clocks on the panel and the lock screen all follow it. To choose a zone for good, run `sudo timezone ZONE` (timezone(1)): with a HUMANVILLAGE volume the file is kept there across restarts (etckeep(8)). The default comes from `initrd/Hakurei/etc/timezone` when the image is built, and `tz=...` in `cmdline.txt` wins over both.

# SEE ALSO
timezone(1), date(1), hwclock(8), clock(7), init(8), etckeep(8)
